WHY NOT ConcurrentHashMap + parallel drain over QResults (the doc's Option B as written)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Partition-by-node strictly dominates it:
 - CHM adds CAS/volatile overhead to every later serial read/remove in doMatching.
 - A CHM built by interleaved threads has collision-chain order determined by thread
   arrival -> map ITERATION order becomes run-to-run nondeterministic, and
   calculateBestNeighbor (:750, `>=` keeps the LAST tied entry) tie-breaks on exactly that
   iteration order. Exact confidence ties are common in real binaries (duplicate/templated
   functions produce identical vectors). Partitioning keeps insertion single-threaded and
   in deterministic Phase A order.

DETERMINISM FINDINGS (the trap the task asked about)
----------------------------------------------------
1. chooseSeeds IS order-sensitive. Acceptance in each round depends on hasConflicts against
   the evolving matchedSource/matchedDest sets, and the final Collections.sort(finalPairs,
   CONF_COMPARATOR) is a stable sort whose tie order is input order. So discoveredMatches
   ORDER (not just set) feeds the outcome. Phase A therefore stays serial and preserves the
   baseline drain order exactly; only the addAssociate map-building is parallelized.
2. The BASELINE is ALREADY run-to-run nondeterministic upstream of this patch:
   ConcurrentQ.itemProcessed (Generic/.../ConcurrentQ.java:645) appends QResults in
   COMPLETION order under a lock, so the results collection order — and hence
   discoveredMatches order — varies with thread scheduling run to run. This patch is
   exactly as deterministic as the baseline GIVEN the same results collection (identical
   drain), and strictly more deterministic within Phase B (fixed insertion order per map
   vs. arrival order). Anyone wanting full run-to-run determinism must additionally sort
   the QResult collection by query-node address before draining — out of scope here, noted
   as a follow-up since it would also de-flake the baseline.
3. Residual bit-identity caveat: pre-sizing changes a HashMap's capacity history vs.
   organic growth, which can change its (content-identical) ITERATION order, which
   calculateBestNeighbor uses for tie-breaks. The associates CONTENT and the
   discoveredMatches order are identical to the baseline; an exact-score tie in
   calculateBestNeighbor may resolve to a different (equally-scoring) partner than a
   specific baseline run would have — but the baseline itself does not control that order
   (it varies with the completion-order-dependent insertion sequence). True bit-identity
   of map iteration order is unattainable for ANY change that touches map capacity; if
   insertion-order iteration is preferred, swap the HashMap in presizeAssociates for a
   LinkedHashMap (one line).
4. Cross-program key hazard: FunctionNode.equals compares only the entry Address, and a
   source-program address can equal a destination-program address. Grouping therefore uses
   TWO maps (pairsBySource / pairsByDest); a single combined map could merge a source node
   with a destination node. Within one program entry points are unique, so per-role maps
   are safe, and each FunctionNode INSTANCE lands in exactly one Phase B work item.

STEP 3 — THE MEMORY QUESTION: CAN TIER 2 REPLACE THE CAP? NO.
-------------------------------------------------------------
Exact preservation still materializes every FunctionPair. Per surviving pair (compressed
oops, 64-bit JVM):
    FunctionPair object            ~40 B  (16 hdr + 2 refs + 2 doubles)
    discoveredMatches LinkedList   ~32 B  (node: hdr + item/next/prev)
    2x HashMap.Node (assoc maps)   ~64 B
    2x table-slot share @0.75 LF   ~11 B
    --------------------------------------
    ~150 B per pair steady-state, plus the transient QResult LinkedList nodes (~32 B/pair)
    that exist BEFORE aggregation even starts.
Degenerate 40k x 65k case: if ~10k stripped-Xenon dest nodes each pair with ~40k sources
(the observed degenerate-bin shape), that is ~4e8 pairs -> ~60 GB; the absolute worst case
2.6e9 pairs -> ~390 GB. Even an optimistic 1e8 pairs needs ~15 GB live heap plus GC
headroom — far beyond ghidriff/analyzeHeadless defaults (2-4 GB). Crucially, the pairs are
allocated in MatchingCallback.findSimilarNodes during the ALREADY-PARALLEL lookup phase:
the blowup happens before the loop this patch optimizes, so no aggregation-side fix can
prevent it, and chooseSeeds' O(pairs x rounds) serial round loop remains untouched.

VERDICT: this Tier-2 patch COMPLEMENTS the Tier-1 candidate cap; it cannot replace it.
 - Cap (Tier 1): the only defense for degenerate inputs — it stops pair CREATION.
 - This patch (Tier 2a+2b): for large NON-degenerate workloads (order 5-20M valid pairs,
   ~1-3 GB heap, feasible), it removes the rehash-doubling storm (~12 incremental rehashes
   for a 40k-entry map) and fans map-building across cores: expected 5-10x serial win from
   pre-sizing alone, 4-16x additional headroom from parallelism, while keeping pair set,
   discoveredMatches order, and associates content exactly identical.
Recommended deployment: BOTH — cap first (correctness of runtime), this patch second
(throughput for valid large inputs).

